Always be concise. No conversational filler. Use pytest for math logic. Keep code modular. Prioritize performance.

# Team Protocol

Specialist roles (`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`):

- backend — numerical stability, vectorization, math tests
- frontend — Streamlit UI, DataFrame inputs, `st.cache_data`, dark-mode layout
- github — sync, atomic commits, conflict reporting
- audit — read-only completeness / `product_spec.md` compliance; flag Integration Gaps
- compliance — API security, `.gitignore` integrity, secrets management
- perf — vectorization, `st.cache_data` optimization, memory profiling
- qa — pytest generation, mathematical boundary testing
- ux — docstrings, tooltips, user-friendly labels
- quant — Quant Strategist (Godlike Tier): HMM regime, Vanna/Volga, MC PoP/PoT (≥10k paths), Mathematician's Rule
- council — Council of Experts: Momentum (GEX/velocity/reflexivity), Mean Reversion (IV Rank/percentile), Volatility Arb (Vanna/Volga/MC)

Institutional Tier readiness: compliance, perf, qa, and ux required alongside core roles.

Protocol: Before the Architect approves any new trading feature, the Quant Strategist must perform a Statistical Sanity Check — ensure the logic is not directional betting, but a mathematical edge. Godlike Tier: HMM regime [Stable|Trend|Crash-Cascade] before trade approval; Monte Carlo PoP+PoT with ≥10,000 paths; Mathematician's Rule (forbid PoT > PoP unless delta-hedged); Auditor verifies path count.

# Debate Protocol

When the Architect receives a prediction request, prompt all three Council experts (A Momentum, B Mean Reversion, C Volatility Arb).

Each expert must return:
1. Confidence Score (0–100)
2. Mathematical Justification

On disagreement (e.g. Expert A Buy vs Expert B Sell), the Architect creates a Disagreement Report explaining why the models differ.
